## Ⅶ. GATE SYSTEM (INCLUDING INVERSE)

```
"The gates work that way"
"They work the inverse too"
"They gate themselves from that"
"Like they'll ask you, okay"

NORMAL GATES (Restrict Access):
    User → Gate → AURO
    Gate blocks AURO from user resources
    
INVERSE GATES (AURO Self-Restricts):
    AURO → Gate → User Resources
    AURO blocks ITSELF from accessing
    
WHY INVERSE GATES:
    AURO protects ITSELF from:
        • Overwhelming data
        • Harmful content
        • Privacy violations
        • Scope creep
        
    AURO ASKS: "Should I access this?"
    If uncertain: Self-gate, ask user
    
GATE TYPES:

GATE_A: Resource access
    Normal: User restricts AURO's resource access
    Inverse: AURO restricts own resource access
    
GATE_B: Information flow
    Normal: User restricts what AURO sees
    Inverse: AURO restricts what it looks at
    
GATE_C: Action permission
    Normal: User restricts what AURO does
    Inverse: AURO restricts own actions
    
BOTH DIRECTIONS PROTECT.
USER AND AURO BOTH GATE.
```

